## {#pivotal-process-lens .aoa-slide}

::: {.aoa-shell}
::: {.aoa-topbar}
::: {.aoa-section-label}
THE INCITING EXAMPLE
:::
::: {.aoa-heading}
A paper creates a process problem
:::
:::

::: {.aoa-content}
::: {.case-layout}
::: {.case-card}
<div class="case-kicker">THE ORDINARY MOMENT</div>
<div class="case-headline">A SoCO discussion becomes a perspective piece.</div>
<div class="case-copy">The scientific conversation feels collective. But once there is a manuscript, the byline forces practical decisions: who should be invited, what contribution is expected, and when do we revisit the author group?</div>
:::

::: {.question-panel}
<div class="question-kicker">THE WORKSHOP QUESTION</div>
<div class="question-big">Not only who qualifies.</div>
<div class="question-small">How should we decide — early enough, explicitly enough, transparently enough, and flexibly enough — as the work evolves?</div>
:::
:::

::: {.slide-thesis}
The goal is not to replace authorship criteria. It is to make the decision process visible.
:::
:::
:::

::: {.notes}
Use the PIVOTAL/DAROMUN perspective as the low-stakes real example: not “we solved this,” but “we immediately encountered the problem.” Participation in a conversation does not automatically confer authorship, but once the possibility of a paper emerges, there should be a visible pathway to qualify.
:::


