## {#guideline-landscape .aoa-slide}

::: {.aoa-shell}
::: {.aoa-topbar}
::: {.aoa-section-label}
BEFORE THE CRITERIA
:::
::: {.aoa-heading}
There is more than one authorship framework
:::
:::

::: {.aoa-content}
::: {.framework-grid}
::: {.framework-card .primary}
<div class="framework-label">ICMJE</div>
<div class="framework-title">Authorship qualification</div>
The familiar four criteria; probably the dominant practical reference point in biomedical publishing.
:::
::: {.framework-card}
<div class="framework-label">COPE</div>
<div class="framework-title">Publication ethics</div>
Authorship disputes, guest/gift authorship, ghost authorship, and editorial process.
:::
::: {.framework-card}
<div class="framework-label">CRediT</div>
<div class="framework-title">Contributor roles</div>
A taxonomy for describing what people did; not itself an authorship threshold.
:::
::: {.framework-card}
<div class="framework-label">JOURNALS / WAME / CSE</div>
<div class="framework-title">Local implementation</div>
Journal instructions and editorial policies may add requirements or operational details.
:::
:::

::: {.editor-cue}
<div class="editor-cue-label">Editor perspective · Ade Adamson</div>
<div class="editor-cue-question">Which of these frameworks actually matters in practice at JAMA — and how do editors use them when authorship gets complicated?</div>
:::

::: {.aoa-bottom-line}
<strong>For today:</strong> ICMJE is our shared baseline because it is widely used in biomedical publishing and it is the framework we asked about in the survey.
:::

::: {.source-note}
Sources: ICMJE Recommendations; COPE authorship guidance; CRediT contributor taxonomy. ICMJE notes that many non-member journals voluntarily use its recommendations, but it does not monitor or enforce adherence.
:::
:::
:::


## {#icmje .aoa-slide}

::: {.aoa-shell}
::: {.aoa-topbar}
::: {.aoa-section-label}
THE FORMAL FRAMEWORK
:::
::: {.aoa-heading}
ICMJE gives us four criteria
:::
:::

::: {.aoa-content}
::: {.criteria-grid}
::: {.criteria-card}
<div class="criteria-number">1</div>
<div class="criteria-title">Substantial contribution</div>
Conception/design; or acquisition, analysis, or interpretation of data.
:::
::: {.criteria-card}
<div class="criteria-number">2</div>
<div class="criteria-title">Draft or revise</div>
Draft the work or revise it critically for important intellectual content.
:::
::: {.criteria-card}
<div class="criteria-number">3</div>
<div class="criteria-title">Final approval</div>
Approve the version to be published.
:::
::: {.criteria-card}
<div class="criteria-number">4</div>
<div class="criteria-title">Accountability</div>
Be accountable for the work and help resolve questions about accuracy or integrity.
:::
:::

::: {.aoa-bottom-line .coral}
The hard part is not reciting the criteria. It is operationalizing them before expectations harden and after contributions evolve.
:::
:::
:::


